      The most common failure in homeowner-installed radiant floor heating is uneven heat distribution: one room roasts like a sauna while an adjacent bedroom stays ice cold. In closed-loop hydronic systems, water behaves like electricity—it follows the path of least mechanical resistance. To achieve perfectly balanced floor temperatures, you must understand <strong>pipe friction head loss, the 300-foot loop limit, and manifold flow meter calibration</strong>.
    </p>

