    <h2>1. The Physics of Pipe Friction &amp; The 300-Foot Rule</h2>
    <p>
      As warm fluid travels through the interior bore of 1/2-inch PEX tubing (actual inner diameter approximately 0.475 inches), fluid friction along the pipe wall creates dynamic resistance, known as <em>head loss</em>. Head loss increases exponentially with tubing length and flow velocity:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>At 0.5 GPM, 100 feet of 1/2" PEX produces approximately 1.5 feet of head loss.</li>
      <li>At 0.75 GPM, 100 feet of 1/2" PEX produces approximately 3.2 feet of head loss.</li>
      <li>If a single loop extends to 400 or 500 feet, total loop friction can exceed 15 to 20 feet of head loss!</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Most residential hydronic circulator pumps (such as a Taco 007e or Grundfos Alpha2) generate between 10 and 15 feet of maximum dynamic head. If a loop exceeds <strong>300 linear feet</strong>, the pump cannot overcome the fluid resistance, flow drops to a trickle, and the water gives up all its heat within the first 100 feet—leaving the remaining 200 feet completely cold.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Calculating Required GPM Flow Rate per Loop</h2>
    <p>
      In hydronic engineering, the required flow rate in gallons per minute (GPM) is determined by the heat load of the room and the designed temperature drop (Delta-T) between supply and return water:
    </p>
    <div class="bg-amber-50 border-l-4 border-amber-600 p-4 font-mono text-sm text-amber-900 my-4">
      GPM = Room Heat Loss (BTU/hr) / (500 x Delta-T)
    </div>
    <p>
      Where 500 is the standard hydronic constant (8.33 lbs/gallon x 60 minutes/hr x 1.0 specific heat of pure water). For an average 180 sq ft room requiring 4,500 BTU/hr of radiant heat with an engineered 20°F Delta-T:
    </p>
    <div class="bg-gray-100 p-4 font-mono text-sm text-gray-800 my-2">
      GPM = 4,500 / (500 x 20) = 4,500 / 10,000 = 0.45 GPM
    </div>

